# Visual Teaching and Lesson Verification

Applies whenever you add or edit lesson content in `index.html` or notes in `days/`.
Layout, TTS scope, copy, and CSS mirroring rules live in `100-days-ml-project.mdc`. Follow both.

## 1. Verify before you publish

The user gives raw notes. Never paste them in as is. Every time, check:

- Facts and terms. Fix real errors. If a claim is doubtful, say so in chat instead of publishing it.
- Missing steps a beginner needs to follow the idea.
- Grammar, clarity, and simple English.
- Order. Reorder into a teaching sequence when the notes jump around.

Preserve the user's meaning and cover all their points. Never invent facts, numbers, or topics they did not provide.

## 2. Decide on a visual every time (do not wait to be asked)

The user will not write "add a grid". You decide. For each lesson ask: is the hard part a
sequence, a set, a difference, a hierarchy, a connection, a code behavior, or a number?
If yes, add the matching block. If the lesson is one simple idea, prose alone is correct.

| Shape of the idea | Use | Existing pattern |
|---|---|---|
| Steps in order, a pipeline | Step flow | `.workflow` + `.workflow__steps` + `.workflow__step` with Lucide icon, label, desc |
| Several related items at a glance | Card grid | `.concept-grid` + `.concept-card` |
| A vs B differences | Comparison table | `.comparison-table-wrap` + `.comparison-table` |
| Code with a result | Input and output panels | `.code-io` with `.code-io__label--input` / `--output`, optional `.code-io__note` |
| Data shape, memory, broadcasting | Cell or matrix diagram | `.broadcast-diagram`, `.broadcast-matrix` |
| Things connected: chains, RAG, agents, API calls, request flow | Node and arrow diagram | inline `<svg>` or a `.workflow` chain inside `.lesson-figure` |
| Real measured numbers | Bar or line chart | inline `<svg>` or CSS bars, hand written, no JS chart library |

Copy the class names above rather than inventing new ones. Add new CSS only when no existing
block fits, and put it in `styles.css` first, then mirror into `<style id="site-styles-embed">`.

## 3. Code heavy lessons need more than a code block

Days 20 to 25 currently have 25 code blocks and no `.code-io` panels or flow diagrams. Do not repeat that.
When a lesson shows code:

- Put copy paste code in `pre` or `.code-io`, never in spoken prose.
- Show the output, not only the input, when the output teaches something.
- Name the lines that matter and say what each one does in plain words.
- When pieces pass data to each other (prompt to model to parser, retriever to LLM, request to response),
  draw the connection. A chain is easier to see than to read.

## 4. Never do these

- No visual just for decoration, and no repeating the same diagram in nearby lessons.
- No invented chart values. Charts only from numbers in the user's notes or from code you actually ran.
- No diagram that simplifies until it is wrong.
- No emoji. Icons are Lucide via `data-lucide`.
- No JavaScript chart or diagram library. Static HTML, CSS, and inline SVG only.

## 5. Keep visuals safe on the site

- Wrap rows so nothing slides under the right sidebar at 1280px and 1440px. Use `min-width: 0` and `max-width: 100%`.
- Visual blocks must stay readable on mobile.
- Diagrams and tables are not read aloud. `shouldSkipTtsElement` in `script.js` already skips
  `.workflow`, `.code-io`, `.comparison-table`, `.lesson-figure`, `.broadcast-*`, `svg`, and `pre`.
  Add class `tts-skip` to any new visual block, and register the class in `script.js` if it should always be silent.
- Give each visual a short spoken lead in sentence in prose, so listeners are not left with silence.

## 6. Checklist before you finish a lesson

- [ ] All of the user's points are covered, with no filler added.
- [ ] Facts checked, unclear notes fixed, meaning preserved.
- [ ] Simple English. No hyphen or dash in user facing copy.
- [ ] Visual decision made on purpose: added a useful block, or prose was genuinely enough.
- [ ] Code has input, output, and a plain words walk through where needed.
- [ ] No invented facts, numbers, or chart data.
- [ ] Sentences and list items end with punctuation so TTS pauses.
- [ ] Visual blocks wrap on mobile and clear the desktop sidebar.
- [ ] Lesson numbering continues from the previous chapter in teaching order (see `lesson-numbering.mdc`), and the sidebar TOC is updated.
